This section focuses on career paths in cyber security and provides guidance on how beginners can realistically enter the industry through consistent learning and hands-on practice.

🚀 How Can I Start Learning Cyber Security?

Many beginners wonder:

“How do people actually become hackers or security professionals?”

The answer is simple but powerful:

✅ Break it down

Cyber security is a broad field. You don’t learn everything at once.

✅ Pick an area you enjoy

Examples:

Offensive Security (hacking, pentesting)

Defensive Security (SOC, blue team)

Engineering & architecture

Cloud & application security

✅ Practice regularly

Hands-on labs are non-negotiable. Reading alone is not enough.

💡 Learn a little every day on TryHackMe — consistency beats intensity.

🌱 Real People, Real Career Changes

TryHackMe highlights real success stories to show that background does not matter:

Paul → Construction worker → Security Engineer

Kassandra → Music teacher → Cyber security professional

Brandon → Student → First cyber job using TryHackMe

📌 These examples prove:

You don’t need a CS degree to start

Career switches are possible

Skills + practice matter more than titles

🧑‍💻 Common Offensive Security Roles

Here are some popular roles related to offensive security:

🔴 Penetration Tester

Tests systems, networks, and applications

Finds exploitable vulnerabilities

Writes reports to help organisations fix issues

🔴 Red Teamer

Simulates real attackers

Tests detection and response capabilities

Focuses on stealth, persistence, and realism

🔵 Security Engineer

Designs and maintains security controls

Prevents attacks rather than simulating them

Works closely with infrastructure and systems

🧠 Key Career Advice (Exam + Interview Friendly)

✔️ Cyber security is a skill-based field

✔️ Hands-on practice is essential

✔️ Daily learning compounds over time

✔️ TryHackMe provides job-relevant experience

✔️ Offensive & defensive roles often overlap in skills

